{
  "id": "u02-l01",
  "unit": "u02-pronouns",
  "title": "He & she",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Bengali pronouns **don't mark gender**: **সে** means both 'he' and 'she'. Careful: its transliteration is *she*, but it's said 'shay' and has nothing to do with English *she*!\n\nThe third person has two levels of respect, just like 'you':\n\n- **সে / ও** (*she / o*): ordinary, for friends, peers and children. **ও** is the everyday spoken form.\n- **তিনি / উনি** (*tini / uni*): polite, for elders, teachers, anyone you would call আপনি. **তিনি** sounds bookish; in conversation people say **উনি**.\n\nThe verb follows **respect**, not gender: সে ভালো **আছে** ('he/she is well') but উনি ভালো **আছেন**, the same polite **-েন** ending as আপনি আছেন.",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "she",
      "bn": "সে",
      "tr": "she",
      "en": "he/she",
      "notes": "Pronounced 'shay'. No gender: সে = he or she. Neutral, also the written form."
    },
    {
      "id": "o-pron",
      "bn": "ও",
      "tr": "o",
      "en": "he/she (spoken)",
      "notes": "Everyday spoken 'he/she', lit. 'that one'. Used for friends, peers, children. Also a word for 'also/and' (you'll meet it later)."
    },
    {
      "id": "tini",
      "bn": "তিনি",
      "tr": "tini",
      "en": "he/she (polite)",
      "notes": "Formal/written; উনি is the spoken equivalent."
    },
    {
      "id": "uni",
      "bn": "উনি",
      "tr": "uni",
      "en": "he/she (polite, spoken)",
      "notes": "What people actually say in conversation for a respected third person."
    },
    {
      "id": "achhe",
      "bn": "আছে",
      "tr": "achhe",
      "en": "is (he/she/it)",
      "notes": "Goes with সে, ও, তারা. Also means 'there is' / 'exists'."
    },
    {
      "id": "daktar",
      "bn": "ডাক্তার",
      "tr": "daktar",
      "en": "doctor"
    },
    {
      "id": "bondhu",
      "bn": "বন্ধু",
      "tr": "bondhu",
      "en": "friend"
    }
  ],
